  "answer_text": "**\"The Mom Test\"** is a highly acclaimed customer development framework and book written by Rob Fitzpatrick. Its subtitle perfectly captures its essence: *\"How to talk to customers and learn if your business is a good idea when everyone is lying to you.\"*\n\nIt is widely considered required reading for startup founders, product managers, and entrepreneurs because it teaches you how to validate business ideas without getting fooled by polite, misleading feedback.\n\n---\n\n### Why is it called \"The Mom Test\"?\nThe name comes from a simple thought experiment. If you walk up to your mom and pitch your new business idea (\"*Hey Mom, I'm building an app that helps people track their houseplants! Do you think it's a good idea?*\"), she will say, \"*Oh honey, that\u2019s wonderful! You're so smart!*\" \n\nShe doesn't say this because she wants to buy the app; she says it because **she loves you and wants to encourage you**. \n\nThe problem is that regular customers do the exact same thing. People are naturally polite and do not want to crush your dreams, so they give you **\"false positives\"**\u2014meaning they nod, tell you it's a great idea, and say they \"would totally use it.\" But when you actually launch the product, no one buys it.\n\n**The Mom Test** is a set of rules for asking questions in such a way that **even your mom can\u2019t lie to you**.\n\n---\n\n### The 3 Golden Rules of The Mom Test\nInstead of pitching your product, the framework boils down to three core principles:\n\n1. **Talk about their life, not your idea.** \n   Don\u2019t mention what you are building. If you start by saying, *\"I have an idea for...\"* you immediately trigger their politeness filter. Instead, focus entirely on *their* world, their workflows, and their current frustrations.\n2. **Ask about specifics in the past, not generics or opinions about the future.**\n   People are terrible at predicting their future behavior, but they cannot easily lie about what they have actually done. Instead of asking, *\"Would you buy a tool that does X?\"* ask, *\"How did you deal with X last week? What did you do?\"*\n3. **Talk less and listen more.**\n   The more you talk, the more you are selling. Your goal is to be a detective, not a salesperson. Let them talk about their problems.\n\n---\n\n### Good Questions vs. Bad Questions\nThe core of the book is learning how to reframe questions to extract actual, unbiased data.\n\n| \u274c Bad (Hypothetical / Opinion) |  Good (Past Behavior / Facts) | Why the change? |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| *\"Do you think this is a good idea?\"* | *\"How do you currently solve [Problem]?\"* | Opinions are worthless. You need to see if they are already actively trying to solve the problem. |\n| *\"Would you buy a product that did X?\"* | *\"What did you do the last time this problem happened?\"* | Hypotheticals are lies. Past behavior is the only reliable indicator of future purchasing decisions. |\n| *\"How much would you pay for X?\"* | *\"How much does this problem currently cost you (in time or money)?\"* | People make up arbitrary numbers when asked hypothetically. You want to find out if the problem is painful enough to have a budget. |\n| *\"Would you ever use an app like this?\"* | *\"Can you walk me through how you did [Task] yesterday?\"* | \"Would you ever\" invites future-tense promises that are rarely kept. Real-world workflows reveal actual pain points. |\n\n---\n\n### Red Flags: Spotting \"Bad Data\"\nWhen you talk to potential customers, you must learn to ignore three types of responses that look like success but are actually useless noise:\n\n* **Compliments:** If someone says, *\"That\u2019s so cool!\"* or *\"You're going to do great with this,\"* they are trying to get you to leave. Compliments are useless. Politely say \"thank you\" and redirect the conversation back to facts about their behavior.\n* **Feature Requests:** If a customer says, *\"I'd use this if it had a calendar integration,\"* do not write it down as a feature request. Instead, ask: *\"Why do you need a calendar? How are you handling your schedule right now?\"* Dig to find the root motivation.\n* **Fluff / Generics:** If they use words like *\"I usually...\"* or *\"I always...\"*, anchor them back to reality. Ask: *\"When was the last time you did that? Can you walk me through specifically what happened?\"*\n\n### The Ultimate Test: Commitment\nAt the end of a good customer conversation, Fitzpatrick argues you must push for some form of **commitment**. If they say they love your idea but refuse to give you a commitment, they are lying. \n\nCommitment doesn't just mean money. It can be:\n* **Time:** *\"Since you find this frustrating, would you be willing to do a 20-minute product demo next Tuesday?\"*\n* **Reputation:** *\"Would you be willing to introduce me to your head of procurement to talk about this?\"*\n* **Money:** A pre-order, deposit, or signing a letter of intent.",
